  "status": "published",
  "title": "Two Wars Are Disrupting Diesel Supplies. Can the Americas Fill the Gap?",
  "deck": "Repeated refinery outages in Russia and shipping bottlenecks in the Persian Gulf are sending fuel buyers to the U.S. Gulf Coast. Valero’s plan to reopen a Houston refinery raises a question for oil producers and refiners across the Americas. Will the new buyers stick around?",
  "category": "economy",
  "categoryLabel": "Economy & Energy",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-26T15:12:42.021Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-26T15:12:42.021Z",
  "body": [
    "A Houston refinery that closed in early 2025 could be processing oil again by the end of next year. Valero has agreed to buy the former LyondellBasell plant and plans to restore 165,000 barrels a day of crude processing capacity. By then, Valero will have to sell fuel in a market that may look very different from today’s.",
    "Ukrainian strikes have repeatedly knocked Russian refining capacity offline. In the Persian Gulf, attacks have damaged refineries while disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz have made it harder to get finished fuel to customers. With both suppliers disrupted, some diesel buyers are turning to Texas and Louisiana. If those orders keep coming, refiners have a reason to expand.",
    "In February, Russia and the Persian Gulf together accounted for nearly 45% of the world's seaborne trade in diesel and gasoil. By August, their combined net exports of those fuels were 1.6 million barrels a day below February levels, according to the IEA.",
    "In Russia, Reuters reported on Nov. 20 that refinery processing volumes had fallen 45% since late October, citing industry sources. The extent of permanent damage remains unclear, and export volumes still vary from week to week. S&P Global recorded higher Russian diesel and gasoil exports in the week ended Nov. 19 despite the renewed attacks.",
    "In the Persian Gulf, crude and fuel are recovering at different speeds. By October, the region's crude exports, excluding Oman, had reached 82% of prewar levels. Its exports of refined products and liquefied petroleum gases were still at roughly half their prewar levels, according to the IEA. On Nov. 25, Yemen's Houthis claimed a fresh attack on Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura refinery. Reuters reported that a source familiar with the matter confirmed the attack. How much processing capacity the latest strike took offline remains unclear.",
    "Russia used to be Brazil’s biggest foreign supplier of diesel. By September, Kpler reported that Russian shipments to Brazil had largely dried up. At the time, the U.S. Gulf Coast supplied more than 85% of Latin America's diesel imports. S&P Global separately estimated that Gulf Coast refiners accounted for about 22% of global diesel trade so far this year.",
    "The shorter tanker trip to Latin American customers helps Gulf Coast refiners when freight rates rise. Marathon Petroleum reported a third-quarter Gulf Coast refining and marketing margin of $43.46 per barrel, up from $14.77 a year earlier. On Nov. 23, U.S. drivers were paying an average of $5.70 a gallon, about $1.86 higher than a year earlier.",
    "The IEA expects the United States, Canada, Brazil, Argentina and Guyana to increase their combined oil output by 1.6 million barrels a day this year. Canadian heavy crude can feed complex U.S. refineries, while Brazil and Guyana are expanding seaborne crude exports and Argentina is increasing light crude production from Vaca Muerta. Brazil’s trade shows how that could work. It can export more crude while U.S. refineries sell more diesel to Brazilian buyers.",
    "U.S. plants averaged more than 95% utilization from May through September. With plants already running that hard, sustained export demand could justify bringing idle units back, upgrading equipment to make more diesel or expanding loading terminals. Valero plans to start with light crude, with a possible later investment in equipment that can handle heavy, high-sulfur crude. The initial 165,000 barrels a day refers to how much crude the plant could process. Actual diesel output will depend on what the refinery runs and how it is configured.",
    "Persian Gulf fuel exports could recover, Russian shipments could stabilize, and China could export more fuel as crude supplies improve. Valero will still have to sell what Houston produces if those supplies return. The test will be where Latin American importers buy their diesel once they have more choices again."
  ],
